This version tries both choices at every day: skip the day, or buy or sell when the current state allows it. It is useful for seeing the decision process, although it is not the version to use for large input because it does not cache repeated states.

```java
class Solution {
    private int search(int[] arr, int day, int canBuy, int n) {
        if (day == n) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (canBuy == 1) {
            int buy = -arr[day] + search(arr, day + 1, 0, n);
            int skip = search(arr, day + 1, 1, n);
            return Math.max(buy, skip);
        }

        int sell = arr[day] + search(arr, day + 1, 1, n);
        int skip = search(arr, day + 1, 0, n);
        return Math.max(sell, skip);
    }

    public int stockBuySell(int[] arr, int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        return search(arr, 0, 1, n);
    }
}
```

For `[1, 2, 1]`, the recursion can buy at `1`, sell at `2`, and then skip the final day, giving profit `1`. It also checks the choices that skip the first day and keeps the larger result.

Important edge cases are handled: an empty array or an array with one day returns `0`, and a decreasing array never gains from a sell action.

Time complexity: `O(2^n)` in the worst case because states are recomputed.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion depth

This follows the choices directly. At each day, the recursion either skips the day or takes an action. When we can buy, buying changes the state to holding. When we are holding, selling changes it back to not holding.

The same `(day, state)` appears many times, so a memo table stores each result. Without that table, the recursion would repeat a lot of work.

```cpp
class Solution{
    int solve(const vector<int>& arr, int day, int canBuy, vector<vector<int>>& memo){
        if (day >= static_cast<int>(arr.size())) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (memo[day][canBuy] != -1) {
            return memo[day][canBuy];
        }

        int result;
        if (canBuy == 1) {
            int buy = -arr[day] + solve(arr, day + 1, 0, memo);
            int skip = solve(arr, day + 1, 1, memo);
            result = max(buy, skip);
        } else {
            int sell = arr[day] + solve(arr, day + 1, 1, memo);
            int skip = solve(arr, day + 1, 0, memo);
            result = max(sell, skip);
        }

        memo[day][canBuy] = result;
        return result;
    }

public:
    int stockBuySell(vector<int> arr, int n){
        if (n <= 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        vector<vector<int>> memo(n, vector<int>(2, -1));
        return solve(arr, 0, 1, memo);
    }
};
```

The starting state allows a purchase. For a single price, the function can buy but cannot sell later, so the returned profit is still `0`.

The input vector is passed by value to the required method and is not modified.

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the memo table and recursion stack

I used two values for each day: the best profit when not holding a stock and the best profit when holding one. For every price, we can either keep the old state or buy or sell today.

The table has `n + 1` rows so the state after the last day is easy to represent. This uses more memory than the one-pass greedy version, but the buy and sell choices are shown directly.

```java
class Solution {
    public int stockBuySell(int[] arr, int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int[][] dp = new int[n + 1][2];

        for (int day = n - 1; day >= 0; day--) {
            int buyToday = -arr[day] + dp[day + 1][1];
            int skipBuying = dp[day + 1][0];
            dp[day][0] = Math.max(buyToday, skipBuying);

            int sellToday = arr[day] + dp[day + 1][0];
            int skipSelling = dp[day + 1][1];
            dp[day][1] = Math.max(sellToday, skipSelling);
        }

        return dp[0][0];
    }
}
```

Here state `0` means we are allowed to buy, and state `1` means we already hold a stock and can sell it. With no days left, both states have profit `0`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(n)`

The price can rise over several days, and the total gain is the same whether that rise is treated as one trade or several smaller trades. So I add every positive difference between two neighboring days and ignore drops.

For `[7, 1, 5, 3, 6, 4]`, the gains are `4` and `3`, giving `7`.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    int stockBuySell(vector<int> arr, int n){
        int profit = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            if (arr[i] > arr[i - 1]) {
                profit += arr[i] - arr[i - 1];
            }
        }

        return profit;
    }
};
```

Dry run for `[1, 5, 3, 6]`:
- `1 -> 5`: add `4`, profit is `4`
- `5 -> 3`: ignore the drop
- `3 -> 6`: add `3`, profit is `7`

The array is only read and is not changed.

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

## Intuition
Since we can buy or sell any number of time, we always want to buy at lower price and sell in on next higher price.


## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution{
public:
    int stockBuySell(vector<int> arr, int n){
        int profit = 0;
        for(int i = 1; i < n; i++)
        {
            profit += max(0, arr[i] - arr[i - 1]);
        }
        return profit;
    }
};

```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:O(n) 

**Space Complexity:O(1)

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int stockBuySell(int[] arr, int n) {

        int totalProfit = 0;
        int minPrice = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

        for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
            minPrice = Math.min(minPrice, arr[i]);
            if(arr[i] > minPrice) {
                totalProfit += arr[i] - minPrice;
                minPrice = arr[i];
            }
        }

       return totalProfit;
    }
}

```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(N)

**Space Complexity:**
O(1)

## Intuition
We need to buy at a lower and sell at the just higher price to buy again at lower and sell at higher.

## Approach
Example 1:-  [9, 2, 6, 4, 7, 3]
- If you buy at 9 then you can't sell at 2
- If you buy at 2 then you can sell at 6 -> profit = 4
- Now if you buy at 6 then you can't sell at 4 which is lower but if you buy at 4 then there is a possibility to sell it at higher. If you don't buy at 6 and 4 is the last element of the array then it means we anyways can't sell this at 4.
- Now we buy at 4 and sell at 7, profit = profit + (7-4) = 7
- If you buy at 7 then you can't sell at 3 so no change
- 3 is the last element so we can't buy as there will be no day to sell it.
- Total profit = 7

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution{
public:
    int stockBuySell(vector<int> arr, int n){
		int profit = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            // If price went up, capture the profit
            if (arr[i] > arr[i - 1]) {
                profit += arr[i] - arr[i - 1];
            }
        }
        return profit;
    }
};


```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** O(n) 

**Space Complexity:** O(1) No extra space needed
